<h2>Core Terminology</h2>
<p>The following terms show up often in the OMC documents. Understanding them up front makes everything that follows much easier to read.</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Slash command</strong> — a special command starting with <code>/</code> in the Claude Code input box. Instead of ordinary conversation, it directly runs a set feature. <em>e.g. <code>/plugin install ...</code></em></li>
    <li><strong>Namespace</strong> — the form where the plugin name is prefixed to a command. It keeps names from colliding with other plugins. <em>e.g. <code>/oh-my-claudecode:autopilot</code></em></li>
    <li><strong>Agent</strong> — an AI worker taking on a specific role. Roles are divided up like exploration, implementation, and verification, so you can split a large task among them. <em>e.g. explore, executor, qa-tester</em></li>
    <li><strong>Skill</strong> — a unit of functionality that performs one task. Behaviors such as autonomous execution or repeated runs are provided as skills. <em>e.g. autopilot, ralph, skillify</em></li>
    <li><strong>Hook</strong> — an action that automatically steps in and runs at a set point (e.g. before or after a file edit). It is used for things like keyword detection and completion verification. <em>e.g. PreToolUse, PostToolUse</em></li>
    <li><strong>Project memory</strong> — a document organized so that the AI remembers the project structure. It reduces the cost of exploring from scratch every time. <em>e.g. deepinit, AGENTS.md</em></li>
</ul>
